nced if and only if Γ is bipartite.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSigned graphs (https://explore.openaire.eu/search/publication?pid=10.1016%2F0166-218x%2882%2990033-6)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 44.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Authors: Thomas Zaslavsky; ... The concepts of balance, switching, restriction and contraction, double covering graphs, and linear representation of signed graphs are treated in terms of the matroid, and a matrix-tree theorem for signed graphs is proved. ... Graph theory, signed graph, Applied Mathematics, matroidal families, Discrete Mathematics and Combinatorics, subgraphs as matroids, Combinatorial aspects of matroids and geometric lattices\n\n# \n\nSigned graphs\n\ndescription Publication keyboard_double_arrow_right Article 01 Jan 1982 English Publisher:Elsevier BV Journal:Discrete Applied Mathematics, volume 4, pages 47-74 (issn: 0166-218X, Copyright policy )\n\nAuthors: Thomas Zaslavsky;\n\ndoi: 10.1016/0166-218x(82)90033-6 , 10.1016/0166-218x(83)90047-1\n\n# \n\nSigned graphs\n\n  *     * Summary\n\n  *     * Subjects\n\n  *     * Metrics\n\nAbstract\n\nAbstractA signed graph is a graph with a sign attached to each arc. This article introduces the matroids of signed graphs, which generalize both the polygon matroids and the even-circle (or unoriented cycle) matroids of ordinary graphs. The concepts of balance, switching, restriction and contraction, double covering graphs, and linear representation of signed graphs are treated in terms of the matroid, and a matrix-tree theorem for signed graphs is proved. The examples treated include the all-positive and all-negative graphs (whose matroids are the polygon and even-circle matroids), sign-symmetric graphs (related to the classical root systems), and signed complete graphs (equivalent to two-graphs).Replacing the sign group by an arbitrary group leads to voltage graphs. Most of our results on signed graphs extend to all voltage graphs.\n\nKeywords\n\nGraph theory, signed graph, Applied Mathematics, matroidal families, Discrete Mathematics and Combinatorics, subgraphs as matroids, Combinatorial aspects of matroids and geometric lattices--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOrientation embedding of signed graphs - Zaslavsky - 1992 - Journal of Graph Theory - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1002/jgt.3190160503)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Thomas Zaslavsky, ... A signed graph Σ consists of a graph and a sign labeling of its edges. ... We explore the extension to orientation embedding of the ordinary theory of graph embedding.\n\nVolume 16, Issue 5 pp. 399-422\n\nArticle\n\n# Orientation embedding of signed graphs\n\nThomas Zaslavsky, \n\nThomas Zaslavsky\n\nState University of New York, University Center at Binghamton, Binghamton, New York 13902-6000\n\nFirst published: November 1992\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.3190160503Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\n### Abstract\n\nA signed graph Σ consists of a graph and a sign labeling of its edges. A polygon in Σ is “balanced” if its sign product is positiive. A signed graph is “orientatio embedded” in a surface if it is topologically embedded and a polygon is balanced precisely when traveling once around it preserves orientation. We explore the extension to orientation embedding of the ordinary theory of graph embedding. Let d(Σ) be the demigenus (= 2 - x(S)) of the unique smallest surface S in which Σ has an orientation embedding. Our main results are an easy one, that if Σ has connected components Σ_{1}, Σ[2], ⃛, then d(Σ) = d(Σ_{1}) + ⃛, and a hard one, that if Σ has a cut vertex v that splits Σ into Σ_{1}, Σ_{2}, ⃛, then d(Σ) = d(Σ_{1}) + d(Σ_{2}) + ⃛ -δ, where δ depends on the number of Σ_{i} in which v is “loopable”, that is, in which d(Σ_{i}) = d(Σ_{i with a negative loop added to v}). This is as with ordinary orientable grpah embedidng except for the existence of the term δ in the cut-vertex formula. Since loopability is crucial, we give some partial criteria for it. (A complete characterization seems difficult.) We conclude with an application to forbidden subgraphs and minors for orientation embeddability in a given surface. © 1929 John Wiley & Sons, Inc.\n\nNovember 1992\n\nPages 399-422\n\n\nCopyright © 1992 Wiley Periodicals, Inc., A Wiley Company\n\n### Research funding\n\n  * National Science Foundation. Grant Number: MCS-8003109\n  * Ohio State University. Grant Number: DMS-8407102\n  * University of Evansville, where I was a Visiting Research Fellow in 1984-1985. Grant Numbers: DMS-8606102, DMS-8808239\n  * Binghamton and by the SGPNR\n\n### Publication History\n\n  * Issue Online: 05 October 2006\n  * Version of Record online: 05 October 2006\n\n\nZaslavsky, T. (1992), Orientation embedding of signed graphs. J. Graph Theory, 16: 399-422. https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.3190160503\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Coding Approach to Signed Graphs | SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/pdf/10.1137/S0895480189174374?download=true)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 32.0 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; The natural bijection between the cosets of this code and the switching classes of signed graphs based on $\\Gamma $ is used to show that the number of such classes is equal to the number of even-degree subgraphs of $\\Gamma $ in both the labeled and unlabeled cases and to improve by coding theory previous bounds on $D( \\Gamma )$, the maximum line index of imbalance of signings of $\\Gamma $. ...   3. ...   1. signed graph\n\n# A Coding Approach to Signed Graphs\n\nAuthors: Patrick Solé and Thomas ZaslavskyAuthors Info & Affiliations\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/S0895480189174374\n\nGet Access\n\nBibTeX\n\nTools\n\nContents\n\nPREVIOUS ARTICLE Predicting Cause-Effect Relationships from Incomplete Discrete Observations PreviousNEXT ARTICLE Generalized Hamming Weights of Melas Codes and Dual Melas Codes Next\n\n  * Abstract\n  * References\n\n  * Information & Authors\n  * Metrics & Citations\n  * Get Access\n  * References\n  * Figures\n  * Tables\n  * Media\n  * Share\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe cocycle code of an undirected graph $\\Gamma $ is the linear span over ${\\text{F}}_2 $ of the characteristic vectors of cutsets. (If $\\Gamma $ is complete bipartite, this is the generalized Gale–Berlekamp code.) The natural bijection between the cosets of this code and the switching classes of signed graphs based on $\\Gamma $ is used to show that the number of such classes is equal to the number of even-degree subgraphs of $\\Gamma $ in both the labeled and unlabeled cases and to improve by coding theory previous bounds on $D( \\Gamma )$, the maximum line index of imbalance of signings of $\\Gamma $. Bounds on $D( \\Gamma )$ are obtained in terms of the genus of $\\Gamma $ and on the number of unlabeled even-degree subgraphs in terms of $D( \\Gamma )$. Numerous examples are treated, including the “grid” (or “lattice”) graphs that are of interest in the Ising model of spin glasses.\n\n### MSC codes\n\n  1. 05C35\n  2. 94B25\n  3. 82B20\n\n### Keywords\n\n  1. signed graph\n  2. line index of imbalance\n  3. frustration\n  4. cutset code\n  5. cocycle code\n  6. covering radius\n  7. genus\n  8. spin glass\n  9. Ising model\n  10. Gale-Berlekamp code\n  11. switching class\n  12. even-degree subgraph\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBalanced decompositions of a signed graph - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/0095-8956%2887%2990026-8)\nciteturn4search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Author links open overlay panel Thomas Zasĺavsky ^{a} ^{b} ^{c} ... View PDF Download full issue ... We prove that, for a complete signed graph, δ_{1} = δ_{0}; more strongly, with three exceptions a minimal balanced decomposition exists into connected and spanning edge sets.\n\n## Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B\n\nDate: August 1987\n\nPages: 1-13\n\nVolume: Volume 43, Issue 1\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Balanced decompositions of a signed graph\n\nAuthor links open overlay panelThomas Zasĺavsky ^{a} ^{b} ^{c}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDFDownload full issue\n\n10.1016/0095-8956(87)90026-8\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n  * Recommended articles\n  * Cited by (20)\n  * Metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe balanced decomposition number (b.d.n.) δ_{0}(Σ) of a signed graph Σ is the smallest number of balanced subsets into which its edges can be partitioned. (A special case is decomposition of a graph into bipartite subgraphs.) The connected b.d.n. δ_{1}(Σ) is the same, but the subsets must also be connected. The balanced partition number (b.p.n.) π_{0}(Σ) is the smallest size of a partition of the vertices into subsets inducing balanced subgraphs; the connected b.p.n. π_{1}(Σ) is similar but the induced subgraphs must be connected. We use signed graph coloring theory to prove that δ_{0} = 1 + ⌈log_{2} π_{0}⌉ and that δ_{0} is analogous to a critical exponent in the sense of Crapo and Rota. We deduce bounds on δ_{0} and values for special signed graphs. We show that δ_{0} is computationally about as complex as the chromatic number. We prove that, for a complete signed graph, δ_{1} = δ_{0}; more strongly, with three exceptions a minimal balanced decomposition exists into connected and spanning edge sets. And we show that, of δ_{1} and 1 + ⌈log_{2} π_{1}⌉, neither is always at least as large as the other.\n\nView PDF\n\n\nCopyright © 1987 Published by Elsevier Inc.\n\nPersistent link using digital object identifier (DOI)\n\niframe--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Negative (and Positive) Circles in Signed Graphs: A Problem Collection (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/313044681_Negative_and_Positive_Circles_in_Signed_Graphs